{"step_index":49,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T23:34:59Z","thinking":"The user is asking about using open-source video generation models like LTX-2.5 from Hugging Face, and wants to know if there are even better models available. They want to know how these can help them make videos for free, and they already have Remotion and FFmpeg. They want a deep-researcher style answer but in short.\n\nLet me research:\n1. LTX-2.5 and its capabilities\n2. Other open-source video generation models that are better\n3. How they can run these locally\n4. How Remotion and FFmpeg fit in\n\nThis is a \"Quick\" depth research per the deep-researcher skill - specific enough topic, 2-3 sub-queries.\nI'll break this into sub-queries: the best open-source video generation models right now, whether LTX-2.5 can run on consumer GPU hardware, and how these open models stack up against Veo/Flow in quality. Time to search for these.","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"best open source AI video generation models 2025 2026 comparison LTX Wan CogVideo Mochi","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Open source video models"}},{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"LTX-Video 2.5 GPU requirements VRAM run locally consumer hardware","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"LTX hardware requirements"}},{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"Wan2.1 open source video generation model quality comparison 2026","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Wan2.1 model research"}}]}
{"step_index":50,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T23:35:13Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-25T05:05:13+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-25T05:05:20+05:30\nThe search for \"best open source AI video generation models 2025 2026 comparison LTX Wan CogVideo Mochi\" returned the following summary:\nAs of August 2026, the open-source AI video generation landscape is highly competitive, with several models offering specialized strengths in performance, quality, and workflow integration.\n\n### **Quick Comparison Summary**\n\n| Model | Best For | Standout Feature | License |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Wan (2.1/2.2)** | Overall Quality | Excellent balance of quality and prompt adherence | Apache 2.0 |\n| **LTX-2.5** | Real-time & Audio | Single-pass synchronized audio/video generation | Open Weights |\n| **HunyuanVideo** | Cinematic Quality | High-fidelity, cinematic, strong prompt adherence | Open Weights |\n| **Mochi 1** | Fluid Motion | Novel AsymmDiT architecture for high-motion | Apache 2.0 |\n| **CogVideoX** | Accessibility | Efficient for self-hosting on modest hardware | Open Source |\n\n---\n\n### **Detailed Model Analysis**\n\n#### **1. Wan (Alibaba)**\nWidely considered the best all-around choice for open-weights video generation in 2026.\n*   **Strengths:** Offers a powerful 14B parameter model that consistently tops open-source leaderboards. It provides a highly permissive Apache 2.0 license, making it ideal for both hobbyists and production teams.\n*   **Performance:** Excellent structural stability and prompt adherence, making it a reliable base for fine-tuning.\n\n#### **2. LTX (Lightricks)**\nThe LTX family (notably LTX-2.3 and LTX-2.5) focuses on high-speed inference and multimodal capabilities.\n*   **Strengths:** Uniquely generates **synchronized audio and video** in a single pass, preventing the common \"sync drift\" found in post-processed video. It supports long-form generation and is optimized for near real-time performance on consumer GPUs.\n*   **Usage:** Ideal for interactive applications and users who need tight audio-visual integration without complex post-production.\n\n#### **3. HunyuanVideo (Tencent)**\nA heavyweight model designed for cinematic excellence.\n*   **Strengths:** Highly regarded for its visual fidelity and cinematic quality. It is a larger model that excels in complex scene generation and following intricate text prompts.\n*   **Requirements:** Requires significant VRAM, making it more suited for users with high-end workstation or cloud GPU setups.\n\n#### **4. Mochi 1 (Genmo)**\nRenowned for its breakthrough in motion fidelity.\n*   **Strengths:** Uses an \"Asymmetric Diffusion Transformer\" (AsymmDiT) architecture, which specifically targets fluid, realistic physics and complex character motion.\n*   **License:** Released under the Apache 2.0 license, supporting both personal and commercial use.\n\n#### **5. CogVideoX (Zhipu AI)**\nAn accessible, research-friendly family of models.\n*   **Strengths:** Strong focus on cinematic, story-driven content. It provides multiple model sizes, allowing users to choose the right balance between hardware requirements and output quality.\n*   **Ecosystem:** Well-integrated into the open-source community, with robust documentation and support for various local inference tools like ComfyUI.\n\n---\n\n### **Recommendation for Selection**\n*   **For Production/Commercial Work:** **Wan** is the current industry favorite due to its balance of quality and permissive licensing.\n*   **For Interactive/Audio-Synced Content:** **LTX-2.5** is the top choice because it produces audio and video simultaneously.\n*   **For High-Motion/Fluidity:** **Mochi 1** remains a leader in capturing realistic physical motion.\n*   **For Limited Hardware:** **CogVideoX** or the \"Turbo\" variants of **Wan** are the most accessible for local deployment on consumer-grade NVIDIA GPUs."}
{"step_index":51,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T23:35:14Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-25T05:05:14+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-25T05:05:26+05:30\nThe search for \"LTX-Video 2.5 GPU requirements VRAM run locally consumer hardware\" returned the following summary:\nRunning **LTX-Video 2.5** locally on consumer hardware is highly resource-intensive due to the model's architecture. Below is a breakdown of the hardware requirements and strategies for running it on consumer GPUs.\n\n### **VRAM Requirements & Hardware Tiers**\nThe official documentation and community consensus suggest that while it can technically run on less, the experience varies significantly based on VRAM capacity[1][2][3].\n\n| Hardware Tier | VRAM | Recommended Configuration / Approach |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **High-End (Pro/Workstation)** | **80GB+** | Run the **dev checkpoint** at full precision using a two-stage pipeline[1][2][3]. |\n| **High-End Consumer** | **32GB+** | Use the **distilled checkpoint** with **FP8 quantization**[1][2][3]. (e.g., RTX 5090 class) |\n| **Mid-Range Consumer** | **16GB–24GB** | Requires significant optimization, including aggressive quantization (FP8) and reduced resolution/frame counts[1][4][5]. |\n| **Entry/Lower-End** | **<16GB** | Below the documented minimum[1][3]. Some users report success with extreme tricks (e.g., heavily quantized models, CPU offloading), but performance is significantly slower[6][7][8]. |\n\n---\n\n### **Key Technical Requirements**\n*   **GPU:** NVIDIA is the primary target due to CUDA requirements (CUDA 13.2+ recommended)[1][8][3].\n*   **System RAM:** At least **32GB** of system memory is standard, as the system will often need to utilize swap/pagefile space if VRAM is constrained[1][6][3].\n*   **Storage:** 100GB+ of free SSD space (NVMe recommended) for model weights and cache[1][9][3].\n\n### **Optimization Strategies for Consumer GPUs**\nIf you have a card with 24GB VRAM or less (like an RTX 3090/4090), you can improve your chances of success by following these common community practices:\n\n*   **Use the Distilled Checkpoint:** Always prioritize the distilled model over the \"dev\" version for local consumer use; it is designed for faster, lighter inference[1][10][3].\n*   **FP8 Quantization:** This is the most effective way to reduce VRAM usage (often by ~40%) while maintaining acceptable quality[1][3].\n*   **ComfyUI Workflow:** Use ComfyUI with custom nodes. You can manage memory by:\n    *   **Offloading:** Offload VAE or text encoders to the CPU (slower, but saves 0.5–1.5GB VRAM)[4].\n    *   **Settings:** Set `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True` to reduce fragmentation[1][7][3].\n    *   **Incremental Scaling:** Start with low resolution (e.g., 512x512) and low frame counts (e.g., 50 frames), then scale up incrementally to find your GPU's limit[1][7][3].\n*   **Pagefile:** If you have 16GB–32GB of system RAM, ensure your Windows/Linux swap (pagefile) is set to a large size (e.g., 100GB+) on a fast NVMe SSD to prevent crashes during peak memory usage[11].\n\n**Note:** If you find your hardware consistently fails to run the model even with these optimizations, the **LTX-Desktop** app or similar tools often provide an \"API mode\" that allows you to interface with the model via cloud resources while maintaining a local-like UI experience[12][5].\n\nSources:\n[1] [ltx.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGsE-rocWEjaox9Oddq6CXr4dibwVl5jL4xqlvcmhiXQo3iQDUg3bM_eyoyDk4MO50E5Pg0GXNy6mADavkNicu2M4yhwbKaI3aOWVZ9kPsF8aE6tI_DGEtr3Z7kY305GvCFypx011EveaClA4o=)\n[2] [mindstudio.ai](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGKZsGOauD1eFCEi4aPuKydfrIRLkCcRKV9Kt9H_vhuHb7fVSyxee8KTIYKqqdnKun-h1gvsedd8owMkiQvnEMTNriVb9Ms0zKOwN1S84GNMqBuEQT0h-T_A5Y9cj11u4Cx6lyR9pcJAH2xCUyBIb_Izdw=)\n[3] [ltx.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGsE-rocWEjaox9Oddq6C